mother armenia stands tall overlooking  the capital yerevan  built in 1967 to replace a statue of  stalin she personifies the close ties of  the former soviet republics bound by  military might but as armenia celebrates  its 31st anniversary as an independent  nation  it's moving out of the orbit of russia  since 2018 in the revolution that took  place in republic square the  democratically elected government has  looked more to the eu and us for support  and while armenia still relies heavily  on russia and security matters it's a  reliance that makes many armenians  uncomfortable  i don't trust russians i don't know why  but i don't trust them because i feel  they take azerbaijan and turkey's side  continuously  i think relations with russia are in  trouble now and i think for armenia it  is better to take a european or american  position rather than stay linked with  russia  like this statue of lenin that used to  stand right at the center of republic  square most of the remnants of the  soviet era have already been removed  from modern armenia but russia remains  its main security ally particularly on  the contested border with azerbaijan  russian peacekeepers operate in the  disputed nagorno-karabakh region and  within armenia itself although they  failed to stop last week's border  fighting  the russian brokered peace process  however is increasingly being seen as a  hindrance and after the war in ukraine  moscow is no longer viewed as an  impartial ally  russia is a new challenge for armenia  the challenge is rooted in their role as  an unpredictable friend unreliable and  unpredictable hence armenia's necessity  to pivot westward just to seek more  balance in the relationship  as the statue of mother armenia watches  over the 31st anniversary of  independence she may be looking to new  horizons  as the sun sets on alliances of  armenia's past  tony chang al-jazeera yeruban
 

